{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-293",
  "title": "Prolog CLI",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog CLI"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Prolog** is remarkably well-suited to play the role of bash in a graph-based filesystem, and in many ways, it's an even more natural fit than a traditional imperative shell. Here's why and how it could work:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Why Prolog is a Natural Fit"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Logic Programming Paradigm:** Prolog's core strength is its ability to express relationships and infer facts based on those relationships. A graph filesystem *is* a system of relationships (nodes are entities, edges are relationships)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Built-in Unification and Pattern Matching:** This is fundamental to navigating and querying graphs. You can define patterns of nodes and edges, and Prolog will find instances that match."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Recursion for Traversal:** Graph traversal (like depth-first search or breadth-first search) is inherently recursive. Prolog handles recursion elegantly, making it easy to define rules for finding paths, connected components, or reaching nodes at a certain depth."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Database-like Capabilities:** Prolog can effectively act as a deductive database. You can assert facts (nodes and edges) and then query them using logical rules."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. **Declarative Nature:** Instead of telling the system *how* to find something (like in an imperative shell script), you tell Prolog *what* you're looking for, and it figures out how to find it. This is incredibly powerful for graph queries."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "How Prolog Could Play the Role of Bash"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's imagine the core facts of our graph filesystem are represented as Prolog predicates:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-11",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "`node(NodeID, NodeType, PropertiesList)`: Represents a node with a unique ID, a type, and a list of key-value properties."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Example: `node(n1, document, [name('report.pdf'), author('Alice'), created('2025-06-14')]).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Example: `node(n2, project, [name('Q3 Research'), status('active')]).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-15",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "`edge(SourceNodeID, TargetNodeID, EdgeType, PropertiesList)`: Represents a directed edge between two nodes with a type and properties."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Example: `edge(n1, n2, 'contained_in', []).` (report.pdf is contained in Q3 Research project)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Example: `edge(n3, n1, 'references', [page(10)]).` (another document references report.pdf on page 10)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Basic \"CLI\" Operations in Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Instead of typing `ls`, `cd`, `mkedge`, etc., you would issue Prolog queries or assert new facts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Listing Nodes and Edges (`ls` equivalent)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-22",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**List all nodes:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This would backtrack, showing each node fact."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- node(NodeID, NodeType, Properties).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-27",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**List all documents:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- node(NodeID, document, Properties).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-31",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**List all edges:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- edge(Source, Target, Type, Properties).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-35",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**List nodes connected *from* a specific node (e.g., from `n2` - the project):**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This would show all nodes directly pointed to by `n2`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- edge(n2, Target, Type, _), node(Target, TargetType, TargetProps).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-40",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**A more \"ls-like\" rule:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`list_contents(CurrentNode) :-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(CurrentNode, Target, Type, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(Target, TargetType, TargetProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "format('~w (~w) --[~w]--> ~w~n', [CurrentNode, TargetType, Type, Target])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "list_contents(CurrentNode) :-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-48",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\\+ edge(CurrentNode, _, _, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-49",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "format('Node ~w has no outgoing edges.~n', [CurrentNode])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-50",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Query from a conceptual \"current node\" (e.g., n2)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- list_contents(n2).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Changing Current Context (`cd` equivalent)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog doesn't have a concept of \"current directory\" in the same way bash does. Instead, you'd perform queries relative to a specific node ID or define a dynamic \"current node\" predicate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-54",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Set a dynamic \"current node\":**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-56",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`:- dynamic current_node/1."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "current_node(n1). % Initially set to node n1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% To \"cd\" to a new node:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "cd(NewNodeID) :- retractall(current_node(_)), assert(current_node(NewNodeID))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Now, \"ls\" could query relative to current_node:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "list_current_contents :-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "current_node(CN),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(CN, Target, Type, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(Target, TargetType, TargetProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "format('~w --[~w]--> ~w (~w)~n', [CN, Type, Target, TargetType]).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-67",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Creating Nodes and Edges (`mknode`, `mkedge` equivalent)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-68",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You'd assert new facts into the Prolog database."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-70",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-69",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Create a new document node:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-71",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- assertz(node(n4, document, [name('meeting_notes.txt'), date('2025-06-14')])).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-74",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-73",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Create an edge:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-75",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "(Note: In a real system, you'd want to generate unique NodeIDs automatically)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-76",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-77",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- assertz(edge(n4, n2, 'relates_to', [])). % meeting_notes relates to project n2`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-78",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Removing Nodes and Edges (`rmnode`, `rmedge` equivalent)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-79",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use `retract` to remove facts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-81",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-80",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Remove a node (and potentially its edges):**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-82",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You could write a rule for `rmnode` that automatically handles edge removal."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-83",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-84",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- retract(node(n4, _, _)). % Removes the node fact"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-85",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- retract(edge(n4, _, _, _)). % Removes outgoing edges from n4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-86",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- retract(edge(_, n4, _, _)). % Removes incoming edges to n4`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-87",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. Complex Queries (Beyond Bash)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-88",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is where Prolog truly shines."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-90",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-89",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Find all documents related to project `n2`, directly or indirectly, through any \"relates_to\" or \"contained_in\" edge:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-91",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-92",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`related(Node1, Node2) :- edge(Node1, Node2, 'relates_to', _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-93",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "related(Node1, Node2) :- edge(Node1, Node2, 'contained_in', _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-94",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "related_recursive(N1, N2) :- related(N1, N2)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-95",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "related_recursive(N1, N2) :- related(N1, Intermediate), related_recursive(Intermediate, N2)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-96",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- node(DocID, document, DocProps), related_recursive(DocID, n2).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-98",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-97",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Find paths between two nodes:**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-99",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This would return all possible paths!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-100",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-101",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`path(Start, End, [edge(Start,End,Type)]) :- edge(Start, End, Type, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-102",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "path(Start, End, [edge(Start,Intermediate,Type1) | RestPath]) :-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-103",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(Start, Intermediate, Type1, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-104",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "path(Intermediate, End, RestPath)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-105",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- path(n1, n2, Path).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-106",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Implementation Considerations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-107",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Persistent Storage:** Prolog's in-memory database would need a way to persist the graph. This could involve:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-108",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Loading facts from a file at startup and saving them on exit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-109",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Connecting to a dedicated graph database (like Neo4j) via a Prolog foreign function interface (FFI) or a specialized library. This would be the most robust solution for large graphs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-110",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Unique IDs:** A mechanism for generating unique node and edge IDs would be essential."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-111",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **User Interface:** While raw Prolog queries work, a more user-friendly interface could be built on top, perhaps with readline support and custom predicates that abstract common operations into \"commands.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-112",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Error Handling:** Robust error checking for invalid operations (e.g., trying to create an edge to a non-existent node)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-113",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. **Transactions/Concurrency:** For multi-user access, a proper transaction system would be needed, ideally handled by the underlying graph database if one is used."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-114",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Conclusion"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-115",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog, with its declarative nature and strong logical reasoning capabilities, is an almost ideal language for interacting with a graph-based filesystem. It moves beyond simple imperative commands to a system where you express what relationships you're interested in, and the system intelligently finds them. It would be a paradigm shift for CLI interaction, potentially making complex queries and manipulations of interlinked data far more intuitive and powerful than what we can achieve with current bash."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-116",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Absolutely! Prolog, a logic programming language, is exceptionally well-suited to play the role of a CLI for a graph-based filesystem. In fact, it's almost a perfect match. Here's why and how it would work:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-117",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Why Prolog is a Great Fit"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-118",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Native Graph Representation:** Prolog's fundamental data structures and the way it handles knowledge are inherently graph-like. Facts and rules express relationships (edges) between entities (nodes)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-119",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Facts:** `node(ID, Type, Properties).` `edge(SourceID, TargetID, EdgeType, EdgeProperties).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-120",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- These directly map to the nodes and edges of our graph filesystem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-121",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Declarative Querying:** Instead of imperative commands (like `ls` or `cd`), Prolog allows you to *describe* what you want, and the Prolog engine figures out how to find it. This is incredibly powerful for complex graph traversals and pattern matching."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-122",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- \"Find all documents authored by 'Alice' that are related to 'Project X'.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-123",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- \"Show me the path from node A to node B that involves only 'references' and 'contains' edges.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-124",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Pattern Matching and Unification:** Prolog's core mechanism, unification, is ideal for matching patterns in the graph. You can specify partial information, and Prolog will fill in the blanks or find all possibilities."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-125",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Recursion and Backtracking:** Graph traversal often involves recursion (e.g., finding all descendants). Prolog's built-in recursion and automatic backtracking allow it to explore all possible paths and solutions in a graph naturally. This makes tasks like \"find all files related to this project, directly or indirectly\" very concise."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-126",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. **Extensibility and Rules:** You can easily define new \"commands\" or behaviors by adding new Prolog rules. This allows for highly customized and powerful operations beyond basic file system interactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-127",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "6. **Logical Reasoning:** Prolog isn't just about retrieval; it's about reasoning. You could define rules like \"a project is 'complete' if all its 'tasks' nodes are marked 'done'.\" This adds a layer of intelligent automation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-128",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "How Prolog Would Function as the CLI"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-129",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Instead of typing `ls`, you'd enter queries at a Prolog prompt (`?-`)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-130",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Representing the Filesystem in Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-131",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The graph filesystem's state would be represented as a collection of Prolog facts:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-132",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-133",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`% Nodes: node(ID, Type, Name, PropertiesList)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-134",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(doc1, document, 'report_q1.pdf', [author('Alice'), date('2025-06-10')])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-135",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(projA, project, 'Project Alpha', [status('active')])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-136",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(data_set_X, dataset, 'CustomerData.csv', [source('CRM'), updated('2025-06-12')])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-137",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(task5, task, 'Analyze Q1 data', [assigned_to('Bob'), due_date('2025-06-20')])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-138",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(user_alice, user, 'Alice', [email('alice@example.com')])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-139",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Edges: edge(SourceID, TargetID, Type, PropertiesList)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-140",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(projA, doc1, contains, [])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-141",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(doc1, data_set_X, references, [])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-142",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(projA, task5, includes, [])."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-143",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(user_alice, doc1, authored_by, []). % Reverse direction for semantic clarity"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-144",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(task5, user_bob, assigned_to, []). % (Assuming user_bob is also a node)`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-145",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Basic \"CLI\" Operations as Prolog Queries"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-146",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Listing Contents (`ls` equivalent):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-147",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- \"Show me everything connected to `projA`.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-148",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-149",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-150",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- edge(projA, TargetID, EdgeType, _), node(TargetID, TargetType, TargetName, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-151",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "TargetID = doc1, EdgeType = contains, TargetType = document, TargetName = 'report_q1.pdf' ;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-152",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "TargetID = task5, EdgeType = includes, TargetType = task, TargetName = 'Analyze Q1 data' ;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-153",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "No`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-154",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- \"List all nodes of type `document`.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-155",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-156",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-157",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- node(ID, document, Name, Properties)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-158",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "ID = doc1, Name = 'report_q1.pdf', Properties = [author('Alice'), date('2025-06-10')] ;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-159",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "ID = literature_review.pdf, Name = 'literature_review.pdf', Properties = [source('Web Archive')] ; % Assuming this was added"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-160",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "No`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-161",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- \"Show all edges *from* `doc1`.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-162",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-163",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-164",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- edge(doc1, TargetID, EdgeType, EdgeProperties), node(TargetID, _, TargetName, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-165",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "TargetID = data_set_X, EdgeType = references, EdgeProperties = [], TargetName = 'CustomerData.csv' ;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-166",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "No`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-167",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Changing Directory (`cd` equivalent):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-168",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This is more conceptual in Prolog. You wouldn't have a strict \"current directory.\" Instead, your queries would often start from a known node or a set of nodes. You could define a \"current context\" predicate:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-169",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-170",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-171",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`current_node(projA). % Fact indicating your current focus"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-172",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- current_node(C), edge(C, TargetID, contains, _), node(TargetID, Type, Name, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-173",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Lists nodes contained by the current project`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-174",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- To \"change\" to another node, you'd assert a new `current_node` fact:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-175",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-176",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This would be wrapped in a predicate like `cd(NodeID)`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-177",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-178",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`abolish(current_node/1). % Remove old current_node"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-179",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "asserta(current_node(doc1)). % Set new current_node`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-180",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Creating/Modifying Nodes and Edges (`mknode`, `mkedge`, `setprop` equivalents):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-181",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- These would involve asserting/retracting facts into the Prolog database."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-182",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-183",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These operations would require careful predicate definitions to ensure data integrity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-184",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-185",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`% Create node"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-186",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- assertz(node(new_doc_id, document, 'draft_memo.txt', [status('draft')]))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-187",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Create edge"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-188",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- assertz(edge(projA, new_doc_id, contains, []))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-189",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Set property (more complex, might involve retracting and asserting new fact)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-190",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% For example, update the status of doc1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-191",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- retract(node(doc1, Type, Name, OldProps)),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-192",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "select(status(_), OldProps, NewPropsList), % Remove old status"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-193",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "assertz(node(doc1, Type, Name, [status('final')|NewPropsList])).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-194",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Advanced Operations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-196",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-195",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Recursive Traversal (e.g., `ls -R`):**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-197",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-198",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`connected(Node1, Node2) :- edge(Node1, Node2, _, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-199",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "connected(Node1, Node2) :- edge(Node1, Intermediate, _, _), connected(Intermediate, Node2)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-200",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- connected(projA, DescendantID), node(DescendantID, Type, Name, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-201",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% Finds all nodes reachable from projA, directly or indirectly.`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-202",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This becomes incredibly concise. You'd need to handle cycles to prevent infinite loops (e.g., by keeping track of visited nodes)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-204",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-203",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Finding Paths (`path` equivalent):**Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-205",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-206",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`path(Node1, Node2, [Node1,Node2]) :- edge(Node1, Node2, _, _)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-207",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "path(Node1, Node2, [Node1|Path]) :-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-208",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(Node1, Intermediate, _, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-209",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "path(Intermediate, Node2, Path)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-210",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "?- path(projA, data_set_X, P)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-211",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "% P = [projA, doc1, data_set_X]`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-212",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Again, cycle handling and finding shortest/specific paths would involve more sophisticated Prolog predicates (e.g., using accumulators for visited nodes)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-214",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-213",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Complex Queries (gquery equivalent):Prolog"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-215",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"Find all documents referenced by 'Project Alpha' that were last updated before June 1st, 2025, and are authored by 'Alice'.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-216",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "#"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-217",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`?- node(projA, project, 'Project Alpha', _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-218",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(projA, DocID, contains, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-219",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(DocID, document, DocName, DocProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-220",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "member(author('Alice'), DocProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-221",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "member(date(DateStr), DocProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-222",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "date_before('2025-06-01', DateStr), % Assuming a date comparison predicate"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-223",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "edge(DocID, DataID, references, _),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-224",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "node(DataID, dataset, _, DataProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-225",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "member(updated(UpdatedDateStr), DataProps),"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-226",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "date_before('2025-06-01', UpdatedDateStr).`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-227",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This demonstrates how rich the queries can become."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-228",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Challenges and Considerations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-234",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-229",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Learning Curve:** Prolog's declarative nature is powerful but very different from imperative shell scripting. Users would need to learn logic programming concepts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-230",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Persistent Storage:** Prolog's in-memory facts would need to be saved to a persistent storage layer (e.g., a dedicated graph database, or Prolog writing to a file of facts). A custom backend would be required to manage the actual on-disk representation of the nodes and edges. SWI-Prolog has capabilities for interacting with external databases and files, which could be leveraged."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-231",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Performance for Large Graphs:** While Prolog is excellent for symbolic reasoning and graph traversal, for extremely large graphs (billions of nodes/edges), a specialized graph database (like Neo4j) with its own query language (Cypher) would likely offer better performance for raw data operations. Prolog could then act as a smart client *on top* of such a database."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-232",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Error Handling and User Feedback:** Providing user-friendly error messages and guiding behavior in a purely Prolog-based shell would be a design challenge."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-233",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Output Formatting:** Raw Prolog output can be verbose. Custom predicates would be needed to format results in a human-readable way, similar to how `ls` formats its output."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-235",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Conclusion"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-236",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prolog is arguably the *ideal* language for interacting with a graph-based filesystem at a conceptual level. Its strengths in logic, pattern matching, and recursion align perfectly with the challenges of navigating and querying complex graph structures. While it might not replace the low-level efficiency of a C-based `bash` for simple tasks, for sophisticated, semantic interactions with a graph filesystem, a Prolog-powered CLI would be incredibly expressive and powerful. It would transform the filesystem from a mere collection of files into a knowledge base you can interrogate and reason about."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}